{"name":"Amazon River","hex":"#777462","fetched":"2026-10-21","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Amazon River","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A member of a mythical race of female warriors inhabiting the Black Sea area, now usually archaeologically associated with Scythian or Sarmatian female warriors.","A female warrior."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A river in South America, that flows through Brazil for about 4000 miles to the Atlantic.","A region including much of this river; specifically, the region of the Amazon Rainforest, or of the Amazon River Basin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Amazon_River"}],"entries":[{"title":"Amazon River","blocks":[{"text":"The Amazon River in South America is the largest river in the world by discharge volume of water, and the second-longest or longest river system in the world, a title which is disputed with the Nile."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Amazon_River_ESA387332.jpg/330px-Amazon_River_ESA387332.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amazon_River","wikidata":"Q3783"},{"title":"River","blocks":[{"text":"A river is a natural stream of fresh water that flows on land or inside caves towards another body of water at a lower elevation, such as an ocean, lake, or another river. A river may run dry before reaching the end of its course if it runs out of water, or only flow during certain seasons. Rivers are regulated by the water cycle, the processes by which water moves around the Earth. Water first enters rivers through precipitation, whether from rainfall, the runoff of water down a slope, the melting of glaciers or snow, or seepage from aquifers beneath the surface of the Earth."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Pirogue_running_on_the_Mekong_at_golden_hour_between_Don_Det_and_Don_Khon_Laos.jpg/330px-Pirogue_running_on_the_Mekong_at_golden_ho